Hello nethack addicts!

	I've been playing nethack for years on and off. Mainly archeologists, 
priests and wizards, with a touch of tourists once in a while. After a 
very promising Archeologist was killed by a typo and carelessness, I 
tried a random character just for variety and was surprised by Puree: a 
lawful human valkyrie. First character I finish the quest with. Also my 
first ascension! I now understand why Valkyries are good characters for 
new players. The starting shield allow for a very good AC early on. The 
Orb of Fate is also very powerful, very useful and not that heavy. Here 
goes her long and spoily story.

	My only conduct in all my games: no artifact wishing. Puree wore an 
amulet of life saving for a minute but felt like a coward with that. She 
always kept one or two in her bag, but never wore any after that. Other 
than that I'm heavily spoiled but don't look at spoilers while playing.

	Played on Mac OS X terminal, 3.4.2. No hearses, no patches. New install 
so very few bones.

Puree the Valkyrie or how easy can this be?

	Her first lucky find was a silver saber in a shop. A +2 dwarvish 
mithril mail and +2 dwarvish helm helped a lot. On the level below 
Delphi (dungeon level 9), a general store and an altar were found. It 
was on this level, after a couple of sacrifices for luck that she dipped 
her long sword in a fountain. The sword was blessed and turned into 
Excalibur. In the store an amulet of ESP was found. Next to the altar 
Puree made her stash. She went back to the mines and found a co-aligned 
temple there. Diving deeper she found the gnome king's cellar, a 
luckstone and some speed boots. In the general store in minetown a 
polished silver shield was bought. She also found a lamp that she 
lighted and that never went out. Practical.

	Mines? Check. Easy. Good AC? Check. Worthy weapon? check.
	
	Back to her stash to sort through the loot. Holy water was made with 
the booze. The single identify scroll found was blessed and luckily 
identified all equipment carried. Now invisible, thanks to a blessed 
potion, she tried to tackle Sokoban, but failed on the second level. She 
never went back. Diving deeper she met a gray dragon which left some 
scales. The lone enchant armor scroll was used to turn the scales into mail.

	Sokoban? Oops. Bah.

Our heroin now had:

cold resistance (Valkyrie)
fire resistance (thanks to a fire ant)
poison resistance (thanks to an unicorn)
sleep resistance (thanks to an elf)
teleportitis (thanks to a nymph)
teleport control (thanks to a tengu)
telepathy (amulet of ESP)
magic resistance (gray dragon scale mail)
reflection (shield)
speed (boots)
invisible (potion)
stealth (cloak)
luck and then some (luckstone)
level 15 (thanks to some wraiths)

	She was doing fine! Her only problem was a dungeon without much scrolls 
and potions. Already she felt unstoppable. She decided to try and tackle 
her quest. She had found the portal earlier. Her strategy was mainly to 
get in, kill kill kill... eat a giant or two. Than get out till hungry. 
Get back in, kill kill kill... eat a giant or two, wait till hungry to 
go further. She lost a couple of scrolls and potions from a mean 
leocrotta - and that was the only bad thing she found in her quest 
(especially since the scrolls where enchant armor). A lot of wands were 
identified by the kind giants demonstrating them. Lord Sultur was a 
wimp. Puree simply teleported in the middle of his cave, wacked him till 
dead, then wacked all his giant friends, then feasted on the corpses of 
the defeated! All-you-can-eat at Sultur's! The best buffet of giant meat 
on this side of the surface! She ate her fill but was wise enough to not 
overdo it. Puree had now a strength of 18/**. Proud of herself, she left 
the ice plains with the Orb of Fate, the bell of opening and a couple of 
potions of full healing. She also now had most attack wand identified.

	Quest? Check. Piece of cake!

	She Invoked the Orb of Fate. Not nowing its power yet. Asked to choose 
a depth to dive to, she randomly said 26. It was very foolish on my part 
- Puree hadn't seen Medusa yet... She found herself in a maze, just next 
to a Nazgul. She had to kill him twice thanks to an amulet it was 
wearing. But she was able to climb up a couple of times and found 
herself at the far end of the castle shock full of baddies. Orb? Could 
you take me back to my stash? Thanks. A lot wiser as to the power of the 
Orb, she slowly dived and killed all sorts of nasties. Zoos, leprechaun 
halls, Dragons, a couple of cemetaries, Minotaurs, Mind Flayers, Liches, 
Dresser? The dungeons of doom are ever surprising to Puree. She 
prevailed. What she didn't see was beehives, Fort Ludios, temples, the 
big room and identify scrolls.

	Before dealing with Medusa, scrolls had been rare. A single identify, 
long used. Just enough enchant weapon for a +5 excalibur. Just enough 
enchant armor for +1 speed boots and a +1 suit of dragon scale mail. No 
wishing yet, no magic marker, a single remove cursed, not used yet. No 
wand of polymorph, no magic whistle, no potions of gain ability. 
Sacrificing gave Molljnir, Werebane and a couple of other non-options. 
She also had no bag of holding. She had enough wand to open a shop and 
be very rich! As much as she wanted to leave this damp and dangerous 
place, she knew her destiny lied below. Cancellation made it possible to 
identify most rings and boots: no levitation. At this point Puree, tired 
of being always burdened, blessed her magic lamp and rubbed it until a 
Genie appeared. Blessed fireproof bag of holding? Granted. It was a very 
wise move: the wished-for bag was the only one Puree ever saw. Alas, she 
never found another magic lamp and spend most of the game 
light-sourced-less (this would be a lame conduct.)

	The Orb of Fate made it possible to do the castle before Medusa, she 
went back again to level 26 and slowly mapped the levels going up. She 
found boots of levitation before reaching the castle. It made it 
possible to pass the trapdoors by the back door. Searching in the 
armoury before dealing with all those hostile people and monsters.

	Castle? Check. Crowdy but smooth.
	
	Polymorph traps had provided Puree with a pet winged gargoyle and a 
vampire lord. Nice pets! Around the moat to the back door. Excalibur was 
very busy with all those hostile people and monsters. After the castle 
and Medusa, Puree's goal were made easier by the finding of much scrolls 
- but no identify, a wand of polymorph and the castle wand. Magic 
markers! That's all this wand was used for. Puree also started reading 
spellbooks. At this point, when she took off all metallic armor and wore 
a robe, she had identify at around 70%. It was useful. She made many 
trips down to the valley of the dead and back up to her stash. Light and 
identify were the only spells she used.

	She almost died at one point. Travelling around the upper reaches of 
the dungeon, teleporting around in search of a floating eye for 
recovering lost telepathy, she started being hungry... and had forgotten 
all food at her stash - now at dungeon level 18. The orb was ignoring 
her pleas to go back, her god was cross with her because of a unicorn 
incident. Monsters didn't leave many corpses on her way to her stash and 
she reached the level fainting. She fainted many times before reaching a 
room where an elvenking decided to read a cursed scroll of create 
monster... It was almost the end of her. Almost. Luckily the first 
monster killed by her winged gargoyle was a soldier and YESS!! he had 
C-rations. [You only feel hungry now]. After this incident Puree was 
never again found without at least a dozen of K-rations.

	Misadventure to remind one of one's mortality? Check.
	
	When Puree had found the vibrating square, her pets were long gone, the 
scrolls and potions she wanted had been found. Tyr had seen fit to crown 
her, give her Grayswandir, bless her with the gift of telepathy and give 
her some lousy spellbooks. L, h, R, ; were rightfully genocided. Not 
before two Mind Flayers had eaten part of her brains and made her forget 
a lot of things. Vlad was dead. Her AC was hovering around -30, Tyr had 
seen fit to give her Grayswandir (now at +7) which was dual-wielded with 
a +7 silver - very fitting with the beautiful amulet of reflection, the 
cloak of protection, the +5 grey dragon scale mail. The wand of wishing 
had provided Puree with much needed Magic Markers and a wand of polymorph.

	Gehenom? Check. Tedious? oh yes. Full of goodies? yes.

	After some alchemy for hitpoints - never a wish from those dozens of 
blessed Extra Healing potions, Djinns were only released when monsters 
quaffed the potions - six times! Properly equipped Puree could cast 
identify at 30%, very useful since identify scrolls were still very 
rare. Puree decided to finally go for it. No wish left, fully equipped. 
Half a dozen of Blessed Full Healing potions in the bag. Blessed 
everything she owned and went to explore the Tower she found in Gehenom. 
Mister Rodney was as easy as a wand of death could make it, she moved 
down to the vibrating square and opened the stairs to Moloch's Sanctum. 
Conflict made the level pretty easy. She never took off her ring of 
conflict until just before stepping into the astral plane. The temple 
was accessed using the Bell of Opening.

	The mysterious force made it very hard to get out of Gehenom. Very very 
hard. Puree was dizzy when she finally got out. Rodney had already 
appeared a couple of times. She ran all the way up, teleportitis helped 
make the trip faster. In her hurry she skipped past her stash and ended 
up at the entrance of the dungeon. Burdened, she sorted through her loot 
and decided to leave everything she thought she wouldn't need. Including 
the very heavy invocation artifacts.

	Dungeons of Doom? Check!! Ready for ascension? For a long while.
	
	Up she went. Confused scrolls of detection and wand of digging made the 
earth plane very easy. Air was fun, conflict and 480hp helped a lot. 
Lightning never touched her. Water was long and tedious. She missed the 
portal twice before finally stepping in, pausing just to change conflict 
for free action.

	Her journey was almost done. Rodney double troubled her just upon 
arrival on the astral plane. No more death charges meant melee combat. 
But it was her specialty and both Rodneys fell easily to Grayswandir. 
She chose to go left, where Death was waiting. Some confused scrolls of 
taming made all the crowd a lot more friendly. Death took her down to 
388HP before falling. Her choice was good, the altar was Tyr's! But to 
her dismay she found out she didn't have the amulet anymore! She tracked 
back to where Rodneys died and found - to her relief - that it was lying 
on the floor. Back to the temple and Tyr was really pleased and rewarded 
our heroin with demigoddesshood.

	Nethack? Check!!!! oohh yeaah...  Still addicted? Yep. Also: Yeepee!

Of some note:

Mjollnir was destroyed in an enchantment accident late in the game. but 
anyway I used mostly Excalibur in the early game and Grayswandir in the 
late game. I've seen many discussions here about the best artefacts... 
my 2 cents: Grayswandir is friggin' awesome.

I didn't play much with cockatrice's corpses. Most found were sent to 
rot in the bag of holding. Except a huge leprechaun hall, a zoo and part 
of the castle crowd. Never heard hissing. Never. I always am very 
careful with the status message whenever a yellow c is around, or afar.

Being used to dying often and in a variety of way I was very surprised 
by the feeling of invulnerability I had. Even the Moloch's Sanctum and 
the planes weren't that hard. I think I'll try and go genoless from now 
on, genociding was possibly what made the ascension possible, but it 
certainly took a edge out of the game. I guess the planes would be very 
different with ;s and Ls around. Rodney was mean but he never really 
threatened me. Demons? Bah! a +7 Grayswandir and a +7 silver saber in 
each hand will mince them in no time. Something else that made thing 
easy was hoarding scrolls of taming for the end game. Only on the astral 
plane where they used... makes it a piece of cake when the meanest 
creatures can't get to you because of some ants.

Conflict was used heavily. Powerful. I had enough gear to try out a lot 
of stuff. Mainly played with possible ascension kit and tried them out 
in Gehenom. I settled on: amulet of reflection, two weaponing 
Grayswandir and Silver Saber, not using missiles (Molljnir and +5 elven 
daggers in bag - just in case,) gray dragon scale mail, speed boots, 
Helm of Brillance, Gauntlets of 25, Cloak du jour (Displacement, Elven 
cloak, Protection or robe.) I had polymorph means but didn't play with it.

Most late identification: wand of lightning on the planes. I used 
engraving to identify it! Even after polymorphing huge stacks of wands.

